1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,600
Everything begins with a bit.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:09,600
Zeros and ones mean nothing on their own.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:15,200
Until we give them structure.

4
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:23,400
Data structures: giving information a shape.

5
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:33,000
An array: elements standing shoulder to shoulder in memory.

6
00:00:33,500 --> 00:00:38,600
Know the index, and you are there in one step — O(1).

7
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:44,200
Address = base + index × size.

8
00:00:44,600 --> 00:00:49,600
But insert in the middle, and everyone behind must move.

9
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:55,600
Contiguity is its speed — and its price.

10
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,000
A linked list trades contiguity for freedom.

11
00:01:05,400 --> 00:01:10,600
Each node remembers only one thing: where the next one is.

12
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:16,200
Insert? Rewire two pointers. O(1).

13
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:23,000
Search? Follow the chain, one by one. O(n).

14
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:33,000
Stack: last in, first out.

15
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:38,200
Function calls and undo history all live on a stack.

16
00:01:38,600 --> 00:01:43,600
Queue: first in, first out. Fair and orderly.

17
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:51,000
Constraint, it turns out, is a kind of power.

18
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:01,000
A hash function turns any key into a location.

19
00:02:01,400 --> 00:02:06,600
Don't search. Compute.

20
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:12,200
Two keys, one bucket? That is a collision.

21
00:02:12,600 --> 00:02:17,600
Chaining, open addressing — each has an answer.

22
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:23,400
Average O(1) — the heartbeat of modern software.

23
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:33,000
A tree organizes data into a hierarchy.

24
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:38,600
Binary search tree: smaller to the left, larger to the right.

25
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:44,200
Every step discards half of what remains. O(log n).

26
00:02:44,600 --> 00:02:49,600
A billion records, found within thirty steps.

27
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:55,200
Out of balance? Rotate, and restore it.

28
00:02:55,600 --> 00:02:59,600
File systems, database indexes — all trees.

29
00:03:03,600 --> 00:03:08,200
A heap: the most important item is always on top.

30
00:03:08,600 --> 00:03:13,600
New elements sift up to find their rightful place.

31
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:19,600
Schedulers and shortest paths ask it: what comes next?

32
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:29,000
A graph: everything is a node, every relation an edge.

33
00:03:29,400 --> 00:03:34,600
Social networks. Maps. The Internet itself.

34
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:40,200
Breadth-first: spreading outward, ring by ring, like a ripple.

35
00:03:40,600 --> 00:03:46,200
Dijkstra: among countless routes, find the shortest one.

36
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:52,200
When structure is rich enough, the world becomes computable.

37
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:09,200
Choosing a data structure is choosing the shape of time.

38
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:14,800
O(1), O(log n), O(n), O(n²) — at scale, worlds apart.

39
00:04:15,200 --> 00:04:19,800
At n = one million: 1 step, 20 steps, a million — a trillion.

40
00:04:26,600 --> 00:04:35,400
Get the structure right, and the algorithm reveals itself.

41
00:04:37,200 --> 00:04:42,000
Every app you use stands on these invisible structures.
